<%= render "layouts/notice", notice: notice %>

<% content_for :title, "Editing category" %>

<h1>Editing category</h1>

<%= form_actions back: categories_path, show: category_path(@category) %>

<%= render "form", category: @category %>

<% if @payments %>
  <h2>Its regular payments</h2>

  <p>
    Every payee found in this category's history, and whether it is part of the forecast.  Each frequency
    is worked out from the payments themselves — how far apart they have fallen — and where that is wrong
    you can say so here.  Naming a frequency also forecasts a payee the history could not: a direct debit
    seen only once, or one whose dates are too ragged to read.
  </p>

  <% if @payments.any? %>
    <%= form_with url: category_payment_schedules_path(@category), method: :patch, local: true,
                  html: { class: "pure-form" } do %>
      <table id="payment_frequencies" class="pure-table pure-table-striped">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Payee</th><th>Last amount</th><th>Last seen</th><th>How often</th><th>In the forecast</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <% @payments.each do |payment| %>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <%# A payee is its counterparty, or its exact description where it has none, which is how
                    the forecast groups it.  Both fields are always rendered, always in this order, and
                    neither is a checkbox: Rack starts a new hash in a `name[]` array only when it meets
                    a key the last one already has, so a row that skipped a field would merge into its
                    neighbour and both rulings would land on the wrong payee.
                    `id: nil` because these helpers derive an id from the name, and every row here shares
                    its names — without it the page carries one id three times over per payee. %>
                <%= hidden_field_tag "payment_schedules[][counterparty_id]", payment.counterparty_id,
                                     id: nil %>
                <%= hidden_field_tag "payment_schedules[][description]", payment.payee_description,
                                     id: nil %>
                <% if payment.counterparty %>
                  <%= link_to payment.label, counterparty_path(payment.counterparty) %>
                <% else %>
                  <%= payment.label %>
                <% end %>
              </td>
              <td class="currency"><%= forecast_money payment.amount %></td>
              <td><%= short_date payment.last_seen %></td>
              <td>
                <%= select_tag "payment_schedules[][cadence_months]",
                               options_for_select(payment_schedule_options, payment.schedule_value),
                               id: nil, class: "pure-input-1",
                               aria: { label: "How often #{payment.label} is paid" } %>
              </td>
              <td><%= payment_reason payment %></td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div style="margin-top: 1rem">
        <%= submit_tag "Save frequencies", class: "pure-button pure-button-primary" %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <p>
      A payee that has gone quiet for longer than its own frequency allows drops out of the forecast, and
      that holds for a frequency you set as well: saying how often a direct debit came does not say it is
      still coming.  Where one has genuinely stopped, <strong>Not a regular payment</strong> takes it out
      now rather than waiting.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <p>
      No payments have been recorded against this category yet, so there is nothing to recognise.  A
      category with no regular payments in it is better predicted by an average.
    </p>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
